you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme global temperatures  tick up as scientists prepared the most  comprehensive  update on global climate research in six  years  more flooding in england and around the  world as scientists say we need  better education on climate change  impacts  and how one city in canada is using  parkland and trees to adapt  to extreme weather events  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions  and we start today with these numbers  here on our data dashboard because  as global warming ticks up and the world  experiences  fire floods and heat waves scientists  are meeting to finalise one of the most  comprehensive  and influential reports on climate  change in  years the intergovernmental panel on  climate change assessment will update  the science around greenhouse gas  emissions and projections  for future warming and as the scientists  meet so too do ministers with the  president of cop 26 alex sharma  holding discussions ahead of the un  summit being held in glasgow in november  our climate correspondent hannah thomas  peter reports  extreme weather events unfolding around  the world  a dramatic backdrop for ministers  gathering at a uk hosted meeting on  climate change  amid floods and storms they are  preparing for a crucial summit happening  later in the year  known as cop26 the pressure is on to  deliver  and keep the worst effects of global  warming at bay  ultimately there is not going to be  anywhere to hide at cop  26. each of us will be in the spotlight  what actual progress has been made in  the last few days  to persuade countries like china and  india and south africa  to do more to reduce their emissions  sooner  until today we were not clear whether  there was this common understanding that  uh  out of the cop there there needs to be a  package of decisions and actions the  homework is not yet done but we  we are we are on our way  there are still real sticking points  rich countries have failed to deliver  promised climate funding  and developing nations are getting fed  up  i've spent so much time  putting ourselves getting ourselves  ready now it's time for action  money is not the only problem that uk  government negotiators are scrambling to  solve  there are dozens of countries involved  in the negotiations around cop26  and a lot of them want different things  there still isn't  agreement for example on phasing out the  use of new coal-fired power plants  now that might sound like a detail but  it's the kind of thing that could derail  the summit  if it isn't sorted out and quickly just  a few days ago  coal was the issue that stumped a  meeting of g20 ministers  climate experts say there's no more room  for disagreements  time is not on our side so that's why  it's so important this is the moment  when we've committed to action  we can take action and where delay is  deeply dangerous  our vulnerability to climate change is  becoming increasingly apparent  a lot is riding on cop26 its success  is far from certain hannah thomas peter  sky news westminster  well joining me now for more on this is  professor sir david king  former permanent special representative  for climate change and founder of the  climate crisis advisory group  welcome to you first of all how  important is this upcoming ipcc report  and what kind of impact  do you think it could have everybody  turns  to the ipcc report as the definitive  document from the scientific community i  i think it is therefore critically  important  and i do believe that what we're going  to see at this report  is a much clearer message  about the dire situation we're in  so i think i think it is important um  but we've all got to remember that the  intergovernmental panel on climate  change  is a an international report  it has 195 nations that are contributors  or  nominally contributors and it's a very  painstaking process  i admire the way it's conducted i admire  those who work on it  but it is not the most agile body in the  world  and so the report itself how significant  a role do you think that will play  in the run-up to cop 26. the climate  crisis  month-on-month gets more and more  challenging frankly we have run out of  time  and we need to be on some kind of a i  don't like the phrase but  some kind of a war footing if we are  going to make real progress  are we anywhere near that i think we  might be in a better place than we have  in the past for one simple reason  john kerry leading the negotiations on  behalf of the  united states of america who i know well  because i was doing the same for britain  in two thousand and fifteen and  uh leading the negotiations from china  these are the two biggest emitters in  the world  china has done in my view much more  on trying to switch across to non-fossil  fuel energy usage  over the last 10 years than the united  states has  but nevertheless both countries are  committed to real action  and these two countries really can play  a global leadership role in a way that  we've not had before  imagine britain leading the way  in the world on on negotiations now we  did a good job  i don't think the paris agreement would  have been reached without that  but nevertheless we are not the big  country that can lead the way with these  two doing it  it is possible and of course they're  working closely with the european union  and with our own alex sharma here in  britain so i  i think there is a better chance arising  from this  of progress but my goodness we've got a  long way to go  professor david king thanks very much  indeed  thank you let's take a look now at the  day's other climate news and  uk housing is amongst the least energy  efficient in europe  but the national residential landlords  association has called  government plans to make rented homes  more efficient a pipe dream  it says more funding is needed if  ministers want privately rented  properties  to have a minimum energy performance  rating of c  from 2025. the proposal could cut the  country's collective energy bill  by 5.1 billion pounds each year  through things like better insulation  and double glazed windows  gardeners at britain's oldest botanical  garden are sowing the seeds for a warmer  future  fig and olive trees are being planted at  the oxford botanics domestic garden  where the rock gardens have already been  adapted to resemble an eastern  mediterranean landscape  rising temperatures mean the familiar  english garden will become  harder to maintain and a cockatoo  species  could soon be listed as endangered  despite being considered a bird of least  concern just a few years ago  the gang gang cockatoo is found in  southeastern australia  an area badly hit by the 2020 australian  bushfires  which reduced numbers by a fifth the  threatened species scientific committee  says bushfires caused by climate change  will continue to destroy the cockatoo's  habitats and nesting sites  the met office is forecasting more  thunderstorms in england and scotland  this week  nearly a month's worth of rain fell in  one day yesterday flooding homes roads  and underground stations in london  the torrential rain followed  record-breaking temperatures earlier in  the week  scientists predict that a key  characteristic of climate change is a  shift towards  extreme weather and recent events here  as well as floods in europe  china and india show how important it is  to understand climate change  so that we can deal with its impacts and  adapt in the future  well let's bring in professor andrew  charlton perez head of meteorology at  the university of reading  first of all should we be surprised do  you think by the flooding events we've  seen in recent weeks in china and europe  and  and yesterday in london were the warning  signs there  there haven't been any attribution  studies for these particular events yet  but we know  physically that we would expect there to  be more moisture in the atmosphere and  for there to be perhaps more of these  extreme events in fact the the latest  climate  projections from the met office to show  an increase in these  kind of extreme thunderstorms and so um  there's certainly something i think we  will need to  to prepare for in the future well yes  and you're part of the climate  education summit which is trying to  address how climate change is taught  so how important do you think education  is in  understanding climate change and  learning to adapt to it in the future  well we think it's it's vital we're  really excited to partner with a number  of  other organizations that see the need  for young people to  understand uh the the drivers of the  changing climate and understand how to  adapt to  exactly these kinds of extreme uh  weather events and we we'd very much  like  uh lots of people to to join us at our  summit will be entirely online  on september the 15th so um if you if  you search for climate education summit  university of reading you'll find us and  please do come along and share your view  with us  well yes and what might people learn if  floods are to become more severe and  more frequent what can we do  to adapt  so there are lots of ways of course we  can we can prepare ourselves and  and our governments can can prepare the  the  urban drainage systems and and prepare  our local environments in cities to to  deal with with larger amounts  of water and also we should prepare  ourselves to  to take um precautions and adjust to the  risk check on our neighbors  make sure we we have a plan in case of  extreme weather and and to  um be able to move our possessions and  those kinds of things  andrew charlton paris thanks very much  indeed  thank you now although we've seen severe  flooding in countries around the world  in recent weeks there are stories of  hope too mississauga in canada sits on  the banks of lake ontario and extreme  weather events there  are being made more intense and frequent  due to climate change  so officials decided to try and mitigate  the risks  here's how the city is being transformed  we have had floods we've had ice storms  significant wind storms which of course  takes down trees  we've had extreme heat  [Music]  we estimate the cost to be about half a  billion dollars to  uh over the next decade along but one  severe ice storm could range from 23  million  to 38 million we have 62 stormwater  management ponds in the city of  mississauga now  and what these ponds do is to help us  control storm water quality  and water quantity we're on the shores  of lake ontario and we have a number of  lakeside parks that doors the water into  the ground  over 40 000 trees shrubs wildflowers and  grasses  are planted annually we are wanting to  plant one million trees  across the city so that again it absorbs  the water  [Music]  measures like roof drainage control  permeable pavement  storm water ponds here what you're  seeing is the energy dissipator which is  the outlet of the stormwater management  pond  i think the key message is just really  um applying a climate lens to decision  making  and leadership i think that you know we  can't do this without strong leadership  well that's everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show building progress  towards reducing emissions from concrete  thanks for watching see you then  you

